  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nWe propose a particle swarm optimization (PSO) approach to minimize the overlap integral C\u2085. PSO is particularly effective for non-convex optimization problems due to its ability to balance exploration and exploitation. The key innovation is the combination of PSO with a local gradient-based refinement step to escape local optima and improve the final solution. \n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nDefine h \u2208 [0,1]^n (n=70) such that \u2211h = n/2. The objective function is C = max_k (h \u2217 (1 - h))[k] / n, where \u2217 is discrete convolution. To handle constraints:\n- Clip h to [0,1] and scale to maintain \u2211h = n/2 after each update.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialization:\n   - Use initial_h_values if available; else, initialize each particle uniformly.\n   - Ensure feasibility via clipping and scaling.\n\nb. Main PSO loop:\n   1. Compute fitness (C\u2085) for each particle.\n   2. Update particle's best and global best.\n   3. Update velocities using inertia, cognitive, and social terms.\n   4. Update positions, clip, and scale.\n   5. Track best_h and best_c5.\n   6. Check time budget.\n\nc. Refinement:\n   - Apply SLSQP on best_h with constraint \u2211h = n/2.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n\n- n_particles = 100, max_iterations = 600\n- Inertia weight: linear decay from 0.9 to 0.4\n- Cognitive and social coefficients: 2.0\n- Learning rate: adaptive based on particle diversity\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n- ~100 seconds per PSO iteration; expect ~600 iterations\n- SLSQP uses remaining budget for refinement\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nEnsure h \u2208 [0,1], sum(h)=n/2, and validate C\u2085 using np.correlate.",
